WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.530
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.290
We're going to booze it up today.

9
00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.450
It is the Booze Cruise episode, everybody.

10
00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.010
Are you ready to party?

11
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:34.290
Season 2, episode 11, written by Greg Daniels, directed by Ken Kwapis.

12
00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.130
Because Angela's been on a booze cruise.

13
00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:37.590
In the Cayman Islands.

14
00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.790
Ooh, I've been on a booze cruise of Lake Ozark, Missouri.

15
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.790
That's the one I want to go on.

16
00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:45.850
Oh, yeah.

17
00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:47.290
Live band, all of it.

18
00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:47.950
Very similar.

19
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:49.750
Okay, let's get into this.

20
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:50.590
Let's get into it.

21
00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:51.930
I will start with a summary.

22
00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.490
I feel like this episode doesn't need it, but I'm going to give one anyway.

23
00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.630
Michael takes The Office on a booze cruise of Lake Wallenpah Pack.

24
00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.870
So corporate won't pay for any more parties.

25
00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:04.950
But Michael found a loophole.

26
00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:11.070
As long as he calls it a leadership training exercise, then corporate will endorse it.

27
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.090
So that's what he does.

28
00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.230
But it's really just an excuse for a party.

29
00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:15.690
Michael loves a party.

30
00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:18.790
All right, let's jump in with fast fact number one.

31
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.930
Booze Cruise was the first episode to air on Thursday nights.

32
00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:25.910
Yeah, this was a huge deal.

33
00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:27.370
This was a huge deal for us.

34
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:28.910
Must see TV lineup.

35
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:32.010
We were so giddy about this.

36
00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:32.830
We were so excited.

37
00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.470
I wrote a whole MySpace blog about it.

38
00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.310
I was so happy.

39
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:36.150
I did too.

40
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:36.670
Yeah.

41
00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:40.970
Announcing our move to Thursday nights because I don't know if people know, but we started

42
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:42.830
on Tuesday nights at 930.

43
00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:46.770
And now we were moving to the Thursday must see TV lineup.

44
00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.490
This was a coveted time slot, you guys.

45
00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:49.710
It's where Friends was.

46
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.030
It was a big, big deal.

47
00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.270
It still is.

48
00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:54.050
It's still their big night of comedy.

49
00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:54.350
Yeah.

50
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:55.670
So we felt like we'd really made it.

51
00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.150
And also, this is a fan question from Sean Anaya.

52
00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:04.610
In episode two of the podcast, Angela mentioned that she and the other supporting cast members

53
00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:07.310
became series regulars during the Booze Cruise.

54
00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:08.510
Yes!

55
00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.570
Angela, how did that feel?

56
00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:16.570
It was such an exciting time, Jenna, because our agents called us and said, hey, they want

57
00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:17.710
to make you series regulars.

58
00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:19.870
We were all so, so excited.

59
00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.490
It also meant that maybe we wouldn't get fired.

60
00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:26.490
Now, did that happen in the middle of filming Booze Cruise or at the beginning?

61
00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:27.390
What was the timing?

62
00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:27.790
In the middle.

63
00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.050
We were on the boat.

64
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:33.430
I remember we were on the boat when I got the call saying that NBC would like for you

65
00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.630
to be series regulars and you're going to have an extended contract.

66
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.690
You're not going to be a week-to-week day player.

67
00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:39.190
Oh, my gosh.

68
00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:40.070
We were all talking.

69
00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.930
We were all so excited, the supporting caSt.

70
00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:46.910
You and I, we had a break where we got to go off the boat, but we're standing right

71
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:48.110
by the boat, Jenna.

72
00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:49.110
We're right by the boat.

73
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:53.590
And I gave Oscar Nunez my camera and I said, Oscar, I want you to capture this moment.

74
00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:58.030
You and I had been jumping up and down, holding hands, screaming because we were so excited.

75
00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.170
And you have that photo?

76
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.190
I have the photo.

77
00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:07.610
And you and I look insane because we're tired and we're mid-jumping up in air, holding hands

78
00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:08.810
like two schoolgirls.

79
00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.710
And Oscar was like, oh, you two are so silly.

80
00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.210
And he gave us a big hug.

81
00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:14.950
But this was job security.

82
00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:15.410
Yeah.

83
00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:22.470
And yes, this was like we all knew now, one, our show must be doing well because now they're

84
00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.250
going to pay us all to be here all the time.

85
00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:24.570
Yeah.

86
00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.850
And then we knew we had a job.

87
00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.010
Special, special.

88
00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:28.510
I know.

89
00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:29.990
Things are starting to turn now.

90
00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:34.530
We've been talking about how we were really wondering week to week if we had jobs.

91
00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.770
And now we're starting to feel like, oh, we're on a TV show that's going to keep coming back.

92
00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:44.050
Now, we still haven't been picked up for season three yet, but we know, okay, we at least

93
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:46.430
have a job for like another 13 weeks at this point.

94
00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:50.770
This was the most job security any of us had had since the beginning of the show.

95
00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:51.310
Oh, yeah.

96
00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:51.650
Yeah.

97
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:52.010
For sure.

98
00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:52.430
For sure.

99
00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:53.410
All right.

100
00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.790
Fast fact number two, let's talk about our guest stars.

101
00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:58.210
Big, big guest stars.

102
00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:03.370
First up, this is the third and final appearance of Amy Adams as Katie.

103
00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.370
Now, I went back and I was looking at the timeline.

104
00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.670
She had not yet been nominated for her Academy Award for Junebug.

105
00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.590
That happened just a couple weeks after we finished filming this episode, but there was

106
00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:15.710
a ton of buzz.

107
00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:21.650
There was a lot of buzz that she was going to be an Oscar contender, and it was just so

108
00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:27.490
wonderful knowing her the way we did at this point and knowing that she had just been a

109
00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:28.950
hardworking actress.

110
00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:30.470
Some things had gone her way.

111
00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:31.270
Some things hadn't.

112
00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:36.650
And to just sort of see this electricity around her, and she is just such a humble, sweet,

113
00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:37.370
kind person.

114
00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.030
You just can't help but root for her.

115
00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.970
And I remember being so happy for her and hopeful.

116
00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:49.430
I was seated next to her for pretty much most of the work in this episode, and we just became

117
00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:50.970
such good friends doing this.

118
00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:57.350
We shared so much about our lives and our hopes and our dreams, and she was just a very open,

119
00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.470
warm, generous person.

120
00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:03.550
And then, you know, this boat was no frills.

121
00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:04.390
No frills.

122
00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.890
No frills on this boat, okay?

123
00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:06.330
Yeah.

124
00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.950
We were just sleeping on those benches between takes, and she was all in.

125
00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:17.330
Another big guest star from this episode is Rob Riggle as Captain Jack.

126
00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:18.270
Riggs!

127
00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:19.070
All right.

128
00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.350
So a few facts about Rob, in case people don't know.

129
00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:23.390
He is a real Marine Corps veteran.

130
00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:23.950
Mm-hmm.

131
00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:30.290
He joined the Marines in 1990 with the intention of becoming a naval aviator, but then he left

132
00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:31.730
to pursue comedy.

133
00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:32.610
Yeah.

134
00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:33.250
Comedy.

135
00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:34.050
Yeah.

136
00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:39.970
But he stayed a member of the Marine Corps Reserves until he retired in 2013 after 23

137
00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:41.730
years of service.

138
00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:45.570
I mean, he's the real deal.

139
00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:49.350
He is the real deal, and the man has such a heart for service.

140
00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:51.370
You do the softball game with him.

141
00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:51.830
I do.

142
00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:52.710
He's one of the people.

143
00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:57.070
He's one of the people for Children's Mercy Hospital, and Rob is just an inspiration to

144
00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:57.230
me.

145
00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:02.570
Well, you guys out there might recognize him also from SNL, or from The Daily Show, where

146
00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:08.530
he played their military correspondent, or from like a gazillion movies, including Furry

147
00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.170
Vengeance with Angela Kinsey.

148
00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:15.210
He was in Furry Vengeance, and then you won't recognize him from a pilot he and I did called

149
00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:19.930
The Gabriels on Fox that never saw the light of day, where we played husband and wife.

150
00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:21.450
We had so much fun.

151
00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:25.130
I keep telling him, I'm like, Rob, someday we're going to get to play husband and wife

152
00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:25.410
again.

153
00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.290
Oh, I would love to see that.

154
00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:28.170
You guys would be great.

155
00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:29.010
Yeah, so you know him.

156
00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:29.770
You're friends with him.

157
00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:30.870
Yes, we are pals.

158
00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:34.310
So I called him yesterday, and he's like the busiest guy ever.

159
00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.350
I was like, Rob, where are you?

160
00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:37.590
Are you in Los Angeles?

161
00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.410
Because the last time I talked to him, he was in Scotland.

162
00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:41.090
Oh.

163
00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:43.190
He's like doing some really fun stuff.

164
00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:47.410
But I talked to him, and I was like, Rob, what do you remember about Captain Jack

165
00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.390
and the whole Booze Cruise episode?

166
00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:50.810
And this is what he said.

167
00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:51.150
Okay.

168
00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:54.930
He said, well, Ange, on a personal level, it was one of the first jobs I

169
00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:56.970
had after leaving Saturday Night Live.

170
00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:58.370
I was so nervous.

171
00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.930
I was really green, and I hadn't done much in front of a camera.

172
00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:07.070
I was scared, and I wanted to do a good job because I loved the show so much.

173
00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:08.990
And the character was so much fun.

174
00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:13.490
He also said he really enjoyed that we shot on the water and that it was a night shoot.

175
00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:17.950
And he said, you know, Ange, I don't know if you remember this, but no one could retreat

176
00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:18.810
to their trailers.

177
00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:21.150
Between scenes, you had nowhere to go.

178
00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.170
So we all hung out in that boat all night.

179
00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:29.630
We sat in the booths and hung out, and people would sort of booth hop between scenes.

180
00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:33.690
You would have people that were like, oh, let's go talk to John and Jenna and Amy Adams

181
00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:34.570
and David Denman.

182
00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:36.230
Oh, let's go talk to the counting.

183
00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:37.210
You know?

184
00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:37.450
Yeah.

185
00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:39.070
And so he said it was so fun.

186
00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:40.970
We would booth hop, and everyone would hang out.

187
00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:43.630
And I really felt like I got to know some people that night.

188
00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:48.130
The second thing he said was he felt really bad for Steve because Steve was shooting

189
00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:54.030
Evan Almighty all throughout the day, and then he would come over to our set and film

190
00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:54.850
throughout the night.

191
00:07:55.010 --> 00:08:01.190
But even though Steve was so tired, he was still kind and thoughtful and professional.

192
00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:06.430
And he said that Steve really became an inspiration for him for work ethic.

193
00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:12.010
Now, if you know Rob, you know he has an amazing work ethic, but he really saw that in Steve.

194
00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:12.870
Okay.

195
00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:16.490
So the third thing he said, and he was like, Ange, this is going to haunt me.

196
00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:19.030
It's haunted me for 14 years.

197
00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:19.490
What?

198
00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:22.910
He said, I had to say Lake Wallenpah Pack.

199
00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.150
And he said, Ange, did you just hear how I said that?

200
00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:26.030
Lake Wallenpah Pack.

201
00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:28.630
I said it like it just rolled off my tongue, like I grew up there.

202
00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:29.050
Yeah.

203
00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.270
He said, I could not get it.

204
00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.890
He said, I don't even know if they had a usable take.

205
00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:36.810
He said, I must have messed it up 10 times in a row.

206
00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:38.010
He said, I started sweating.

207
00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:39.190
I was so embarrassed.

208
00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:39.870
Oh, no.

209
00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.770
He said, I could not say Wallenpah Pack.

210
00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:48.590
And he said, when his shoot was done and he was driving home, all he did on his drive

211
00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.690
home from Long Beach, it was like an hour and a half drive.

212
00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:53.390
He was like, Lake Wallenpah Pack.

213
00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:54.150
Wallenpah Pack.

214
00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.850
Say it with no problem in my car driving home.

215
00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.150
He said, but I could not say it in the take.

216
00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:04.770
And he said, and it's haunted me for 14 years.

217
00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:06.190
Rob, I love you.

218
00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:17.370
And he wanted me to let you guys know that it's going to air after Naked and Afraid.

219
00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:19.990
I said, well, Rob, that's a lead in.

220
00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:23.010
Rob is fantastic and I adore him.

221
00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:23.910
Thanks so much, Rob.

222
00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:25.190
Oh, that was amazing.

223
00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:26.930
Good intel, Ange.

224
00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:31.150
So we have yet another guest star on this episode.

225
00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:37.790
Brenda Withers plays Brenda, the corporate liaison sent on the booze cruise to keep tabs

226
00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:38.610
on Michael.

227
00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:40.930
And there's some fun facts about Brenda.

228
00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:42.250
Let's hear them.

229
00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:47.450
Brenda and Mindy Kaling were a writing team before The Office.

230
00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:54.170
They wrote and performed in an off-Broadway play called Matt and Ben, which was about

231
00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:57.090
the friendship of Matt Damon and Ben Affleck.

232
00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:58.670
And they played Matt and Ben.

233
00:09:58.850 --> 00:09:59.230
Yes.

234
00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:01.830
Brenda played Matt and Mindy played Ben.

235
00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:08.570
It was set in 1995 and it tells the story of Matt and Ben before they were stars in their

236
00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:11.090
apartment fantasizing about being famous.

237
00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:13.570
It was a huge hit.

238
00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:15.210
It was a smash critical hit.

239
00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:15.810
I would have loved to have seen that.

240
00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:18.870
I looked up the New York Times review and it was just glowing.

241
00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:22.390
And this really put them on the map.

242
00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:25.830
And it's definitely what got Mindy noticed.

243
00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:27.830
We haven't even talked about this.

244
00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:30.710
We haven't even mentioned who's coming on the show today.

245
00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:31.390
I know.

246
00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:35.150
I was just about to let it roll off my tongue as if we had announced it.

247
00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:35.530
I know.

248
00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:36.410
I thought about it too.

249
00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:37.850
And I was like, wait, we didn't even say.

250
00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:43.470
You guys, we have an amazingly huge, awesome, the guest of all guests is on today.

251
00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:48.750
Greg Daniels, the creator of the show, is coming on the episode today.

252
00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:50.090
He's going to be here any minute.

253
00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:52.730
He is going to sit with us.

254
00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:54.990
We're a little giddy, as you can tell.

255
00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:56.530
We're super giddy.

256
00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:58.990
And I believe he went and saw this show.

257
00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:00.470
And that's how he discovered Mindy.

258
00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:01.750
Yeah, we'll have to ask him.

259
00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:03.170
We'll ask him because he'll be here.

260
00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:04.350
Because he's going to be here.

261
00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:05.450
Yes, that's right.

262
00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:09.830
Greg Daniels, showrunner of The Office and writer of this episode, will be joining us.

263
00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:10.630
That's hilarious.

264
00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.410
Why did we not lead with that?

265
00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:13.910
Talk about burying the lead.

266
00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.110
I think we're just so excited.

267
00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:18.650
I know I'm watching the clock because he's going to be here soon.

268
00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:21.730
So I was like, oh, let's get Fast Facts done because then Greg's going to be here.

269
00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:23.610
Oh, just get Fast Facts done.

270
00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:24.310
Hey, now.

271
00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:26.130
Hurry up and get your Fast Facts done.

272
00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:27.730
I had a good tip in for your Fast Facts.

273
00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:28.310
No, I'm kidding.

274
00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:28.830
I'm kidding.

275
00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:29.350
All right.

276
00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:31.930
Well, let's finish with these Fast Facts so that Greg can get in here.

277
00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:37.750
Fast Fact number three, we shot on location, guys, on a boat.

278
00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:39.170
On a boat.

279
00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:40.010
On a boat.

280
00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:45.050
This was outside of the Long Beach Harbor, which is about 45 minutes south of Los Angeles.

281
00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.270
We shot there for three days.

282
00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:51.150
Now, for two of the days, we filmed while the boat was docked.

283
00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:54.150
And then there was one night when we were moving.

284
00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:59.250
Because we were in Long Beach and they put us up at a hotel, I had these grand fantasies

285
00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:06.090
that we were going to be all hanging out back at the hotel and kind of partying or something.

286
00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:06.370
I don't know.

287
00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:07.250
I had this idea.

288
00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:09.230
Yeah, I'd never really been on location before.

289
00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:10.610
We never even saw the hotel.

290
00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:14.310
Literally, we were on the boat all night and we would go to the hotel and maybe sleep a few hours

291
00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:15.330
and then we'd go right back.

292
00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:17.150
I have no memory of the hotel.

293
00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:22.670
Well, no, but I did bring poker chips thinking that we would hang out at the hotel and play

294
00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:24.130
poker, but it never happened.

295
00:12:24.710 --> 00:12:29.070
We actually filmed from like late afternoon overnight until the sun came up.

296
00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:33.590
Yes, I wrote in my journal that we filmed from 2.30 p.m.

297
00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:35.510
and usually wrapped around 5 a.m.

298
00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:40.050
And I wrote, I felt the motion of that boat all week the next week.

299
00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:43.290
I would be in line at the grocery store and realize I was swaying.

300
00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:44.630
Oh my gosh.

301
00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:48.870
It was intense because even when the boat wasn't moving, you're still on water.

302
00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:53.330
Well, yeah, even, I actually think it's worse when you're just docked because then it's

303
00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:57.830
like doing this weird like, like, kind of thing.

304
00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:00.810
And when you're moving, you know, it's better.

305
00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:06.250
It's like a forward motion, but that kind of rocking against the dock for days on it.

306
00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:07.170
Oh my gosh.

307
00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:07.970
Oh my gosh.

308
00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:08.890
It's bringing back memories.

309
00:13:09.130 --> 00:13:12.890
I have some fantastic photos from this episode because I brought my camera.

310
00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.330
My old school camera.

311
00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:18.590
I already mentioned we got a photo when I found out I was series regular, but I had it

312
00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:19.910
on the boat with me the whole time.

313
00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:25.070
So I have, I have so many, I was looking at all my office photos, all my memorabilia

314
00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:27.730
and Booze Cruise is a huge chunk of photos.

315
00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:29.830
I brought my camcorder.

316
00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:36.030
Back then we didn't have iPhones on this boat, but I had a little camcorder and I filmed

317
00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:37.690
all of us behind the scenes.

318
00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:38.810
I remember this.

319
00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:41.830
A documentary of Booze Cruise, which you can find on YouTube.

320
00:13:42.190 --> 00:13:47.390
If you go on YouTube and like Jenna Fisher Booze Cruise documentary, it'll pop up.

321
00:13:47.490 --> 00:13:48.270
It's amazing.

322
00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:50.230
I interviewed crew members.

323
00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:52.050
I asked them what they did.

324
00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:56.290
And it's very funny because you see us getting on the boat.

325
00:13:56.450 --> 00:14:00.030
Dave Rogers, our editor, I gave him all the footage and he put together this video.

326
00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:02.130
You see us all getting on the boat.

327
00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:04.470
And we're like, yeah, the Booze Cruise.

328
00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:07.510
And then the first night we're like, yeah, Booze Cruise.

329
00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:08.670
We're still, we're doing it.

330
00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:09.090
We're doing it.

331
00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:15.110
Like by the third night, no one will speak to me, but you definitely see the progression.

332
00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:18.390
I watched the whole thing and it just made me smile.

333
00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:21.210
I just, man, it just brought me right back.

334
00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:23.130
And we also look 12 years old.

335
00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:24.050
We do.

336
00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:29.190
We just look so, I was like, oh man, time has marched on across my face.

337
00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:30.290
Oh no.

338
00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:32.830
I was like, wow, we look so young.

339
00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:34.490
Well, that's all I've got.

340
00:14:35.050 --> 00:14:38.110
Should we go get Greg and bring him in here and break down this episode?

341
00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.790
Yes, because I have a ton of note cards.

342
00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:42.070
You really do.

343
00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:42.830
I really do.

344
00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:51.130
Angela guys got here early because she had so many note cards that she was like, I came in and I was shocked.

345
00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:52.790
I said, what is this?

346
00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:54.190
Here it is though.

347
00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:55.410
All right, well, let's do it.

348
00:14:55.410 --> 00:15:03.680
Hi, I'm Jessica St. Clair and I'm June Diane Raphael.

349
00:15:03.820 --> 00:15:09.940
And we are two friends trying to survive the chaos and celebrate the joy that life throws our way.

350
00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:13.200
And we do it every week on our podcast, The Deep Dive.

351
00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:17.660
Sometimes we dig into the deep stuff, like how I communicate with my dead best friend.

352
00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:21.700
And sometimes we give bad advice based off a TikTok I saw.

353
00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:23.780
And we're not going to apologize for that.

354
00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:24.860
Absolutely not.

355
00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:28.440
You'll laugh, you'll cry, you'll hire a psychic medium.

356
00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:29.820
Join us, won't you?

357
00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:33.520
Listen to The Deep Dive wherever you get your podcasts from Lemonada Media.

358
00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:42.310
Hey guys, we're back from our break.

359
00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:44.910
And we are sitting here with Greg Daniels.

360
00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:45.490
Yay!

361
00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:48.970
Writer of Booze Cruise, showrunner of The Office.

362
00:15:49.330 --> 00:15:51.550
Greg, thank you so much for coming in.

363
00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:53.110
Well, thank you so much for inviting me.

364
00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:54.250
I love your show.

365
00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:55.550
You guys are so good together.

366
00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:57.270
It's so entertaining to hear you.

367
00:15:57.410 --> 00:16:02.090
And it's so fun for me to hear your voices and your points of view and everything.

368
00:16:02.090 --> 00:16:03.870
Thank you so much, Greg.

369
00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:07.010
If not for you, Greg, we would not be best friends and this show wouldn't exiSt.

370
00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:10.630
Our lives would be completely – I don't even know.

371
00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:14.230
I can't even put it into words, honestly, because now I'll get emotional.

372
00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:14.790
Here I go.

373
00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:15.850
Angela's crying already.

374
00:16:16.470 --> 00:16:17.270
I can't.

375
00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:17.650
Sorry.

376
00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:19.250
It just all kind of hit me.

377
00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:27.650
Do you know how Jim says he'll bet a paycheck that Michael in this episode is going to do the Titanic thing?

378
00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:28.030
Yeah.

379
00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:30.630
I would have said Angela was going to tear up.

380
00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:33.430
And it happened already.

381
00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:34.410
It happened so soon.

382
00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:34.830
I know.

383
00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:35.510
I'm sorry.

384
00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:42.730
I just – it just all – like the wave hit me of just gratitude I have for you, just absolute gratitude in my heart.

385
00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:43.250
Likewise.

386
00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:43.830
Yeah, you.

387
00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:46.030
It was a great time for all of us.

388
00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:50.610
And if your friendship was the only thing that came out of it, that would be cool.

389
00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:51.230
Aw.

390
00:16:51.370 --> 00:16:52.630
And yet it's not, is it?

391
00:16:55.010 --> 00:17:01.470
Well, Greg, when we have guests, we always like to start with asking them, how did they end up being on The Office?

392
00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:04.650
How did you end up developing The Office for American Television?

393
00:17:05.050 --> 00:17:13.290
I will go back a little ways because I was kind of already with a career at the time that the show started.

394
00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.490
You had a very successful career, Greg.

395
00:17:15.830 --> 00:17:20.150
I mean, you had done King the – I won't list your IMDb, but you were the real deal.

396
00:17:20.350 --> 00:17:20.870
Go ahead.

397
00:17:21.470 --> 00:17:22.510
People don't know it enough.

398
00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:24.390
You created King of the Hill.

399
00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:24.830
King of the Hill.

400
00:17:24.850 --> 00:17:25.910
And you were running that show.

401
00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:26.570
Yeah, yeah.

402
00:17:26.570 --> 00:17:29.230
But you had written on Saturday Night Live and –

403
00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:29.630
Simpsons.

404
00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:30.850
The Simpsons forever.

405
00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:34.930
And oh, my gosh, there's that one song that you wrote on The Simpsons that is just amazing.

406
00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:36.170
Anyway, okay, okay.

407
00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:37.070
Who needs a quickie mart?

408
00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:38.890
Yes, who needs – yes, yes.

409
00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:41.710
Okay, so you were the real deal.

410
00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:43.090
It was – things were cooking.

411
00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:43.910
So, yeah.

412
00:17:44.010 --> 00:17:45.610
So I was in a good position.

413
00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:50.090
You know how they say like you need 10,000 hours to be good at something?

414
00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:50.550
Yes.

415
00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:55.530
So I had sort of 10,000 hours on The Simpsons and Saturday Night Live, and then I had another

416
00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:57.470
like 15,000 hours on King of the Hill.

417
00:17:57.570 --> 00:17:59.230
So I was like prepared.

418
00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:08.450
And what happened was my agent sent me the tape of The Office, and it was pretty unknown

419
00:18:08.450 --> 00:18:08.950
over here.

420
00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:10.710
And it had a pretty boring title.

421
00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:12.670
And I didn't watch it.

422
00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:14.490
The BBC version.

423
00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:15.610
The BBC version, yes.

424
00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:17.350
And so he called me.

425
00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:21.290
It was over Christmas, like two years before our show came out.

426
00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:25.230
And he said, all right, I'm going to send this to somebody else if you don't watch it.

427
00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:26.670
And I was like, oh, okay, hang on, hang on.

428
00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:27.370
I'll watch it tonight.

429
00:18:27.790 --> 00:18:31.450
And I put it in around 11, and I stayed up till like 3 in the morning watching it.

430
00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:33.070
I thought it was just brilliant.

431
00:18:34.790 --> 00:18:38.670
So I called, and I said, I really want to meet these guys.

432
00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:46.710
And I had been kind of like a good little student always in my career.

433
00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:49.510
And I always wanted to interact with people who I respected.

434
00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:54.950
And I sold an episode to Seinfeld so I could work with Larry David.

435
00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:57.830
And that was like a career important move for me.

436
00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:02.230
And I kind of identified that Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant had done something that

437
00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:06.530
put them in the top ranks of people working in this profession.

438
00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:07.810
So I really wanted to meet them.

439
00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:10.430
And I didn't really think it could be adapted.

440
00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:12.330
So I was kind of scamming them.

441
00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.110
I was like, I'm going to go meet you and take this interesting meeting.

442
00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:16.690
Take your knowledge from you.

443
00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:17.450
Ask you questions.

444
00:19:17.630 --> 00:19:18.570
Build a relationship.

445
00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:20.210
Maybe for something in the future.

446
00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:20.770
You never know.

447
00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:21.630
What's not going to be this?

448
00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:26.730
And anyway, so we met over at Ben Silverman's office because he had the rights.

449
00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:33.850
And we got along very well because it turned out that they loved American TV, and specifically

450
00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:34.590
The Simpsons.

451
00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:40.210
And specifically, their favorite episode, or Ricky's favorite episode, was Homer Badman,

452
00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:40.910
which I had written.

453
00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:42.650
And so we kind of hit it off.

454
00:19:42.650 --> 00:19:43.270
That's amazing.

455
00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:52.730
And I was telling them all the kind of theories that I had about making a TV show that I put

456
00:19:52.730 --> 00:20:01.150
into place in King of the Hill, which was a slower pace and allowing for awkwardness and

457
00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:02.710
realism and all this stuff.

458
00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.590
And they really responded to that.

459
00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:12.270
And so we decided, well, maybe I would be the person to adapt it into America.

460
00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:20.710
And then there was a couple of months where we were kind of hammering it out.

461
00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:28.270
But it was always, the thought was always to bring it to someplace cool and cable-y because

462
00:20:28.270 --> 00:20:30.750
the show was not like what was on TV.

463
00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:36.910
I think the biggest show at the time was Will and Grace, which was very kind of multi-camera,

464
00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:37.890
fast-paced.

465
00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:38.410
Far more broad.

466
00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:38.830
Yes.

467
00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:40.070
Yeah, very different feel.

468
00:20:40.270 --> 00:20:42.970
Yeah, it didn't have a network feel of television.

469
00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:44.350
Yeah, it felt weird.

470
00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:48.070
And so we were talking about, well, maybe we'll go to HBO.

471
00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:52.090
And apparently Kevin Reilly at FX really likes it.

472
00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:54.770
And so we had a couple of thoughts.

473
00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:57.010
And anyway, so I signed on to do it.

474
00:20:57.390 --> 00:21:00.350
And then Kevin Reilly left FX and went to NBC.

475
00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:05.670
And HBO didn't want to do it because they didn't want to do a remake.

476
00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:10.130
And Ben was like, you got to sell this to NBC.

477
00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:15.930
And then I got very worried because I was like, this is, I got to work on NBC, you know?

478
00:21:16.150 --> 00:21:17.890
You're like, this isn't what I signed up for.

479
00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:18.160
Yeah.

480
00:21:18.410 --> 00:21:19.410
Cool cable show.

481
00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:20.230
Yeah, exactly.

482
00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:27.750
And I also had these stress streams where I would be put on trial by all the comedy nerds

483
00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:32.710
in the world for doing this lousy NBC version of The Office, you know?

484
00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:33.650
Yeah.

485
00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:42.110
Yeah, so, but eventually we were like, well, maybe the value of it will be to move network

486
00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:49.610
comedy into a new direction, maybe open it up just a little bit and make it more like what

487
00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:55.790
I liked about animation, which was single camera and didn't have a laugh track and stuff like

488
00:21:55.790 --> 00:21:55.990
that.

489
00:21:56.070 --> 00:22:00.550
So I was like, okay, I kind of convinced myself it was worth doing just for that.

490
00:22:00.750 --> 00:22:03.470
And it ushered in this whole new type of American television.

491
00:22:03.470 --> 00:22:03.910
Completely.

492
00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:07.270
All right, well, let's get into Booze Cruise.

493
00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:08.710
All right, let's do it, Jenna.

494
00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:11.650
What was the inspiration for this episode?

495
00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:17.330
You know, one of the things we were doing was we were looking for classic office situations

496
00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:22.250
and trying to test them for, was there comic potential?

497
00:22:22.490 --> 00:22:28.010
