We're sponsored by.

1005
00:42:51.810 --> 00:42:51.990
Yeah.

1006
00:42:52.390 --> 00:42:53.410
Sold by Jan.

1007
00:42:53.830 --> 00:42:55.430
Jan's essential oils and candles.

1008
00:42:55.730 --> 00:42:56.730
So here's what it has in it.

1009
00:42:57.110 --> 00:43:07.490
Lavender, peppermint, chamomile, something called ylang-ylang, birch, and then it has frankincense and myrrh.

1010
00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:10.210
That's the stuff that the wise men gave to Jesus.

1011
00:43:10.530 --> 00:43:10.970
Yeah, I know.

1012
00:43:11.070 --> 00:43:11.830
Two of the things.

1013
00:43:12.170 --> 00:43:13.030
Very expensive stuff.

1014
00:43:13.150 --> 00:43:13.450
Yeah.

1015
00:43:13.670 --> 00:43:13.850
Yeah.

1016
00:43:14.290 --> 00:43:14.810
Fancy.

1017
00:43:15.070 --> 00:43:15.550
Who knew?

1018
00:43:18.190 --> 00:43:20.310
Circumstance and myrrh are real, but.

1019
00:43:20.430 --> 00:43:21.470
I didn't know they were real.

1020
00:43:21.770 --> 00:43:22.150
I mean.

1021
00:43:22.570 --> 00:43:24.510
I thought they were part of a Christmas song.

1022
00:43:24.970 --> 00:43:25.950
That's not real.

1023
00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:28.290
But like, what are they?

1024
00:43:28.470 --> 00:43:30.950
I guess they are what you rub behind your ears when you're seasick.

1025
00:43:30.950 --> 00:43:34.350
It sounds like they just threw everything in the kitchen sink into this.

1026
00:43:34.450 --> 00:43:34.710
Yeah.

1027
00:43:34.950 --> 00:43:35.000
They did.

1028
00:43:35.410 --> 00:43:36.610
The ylang-ylang?

1029
00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:37.430
I don't know.

1030
00:43:37.650 --> 00:43:37.910
Ylang-ylang.

1031
00:43:37.970 --> 00:43:38.830
Ylang-ylang.

1032
00:43:39.110 --> 00:43:39.530
All right.

1033
00:43:39.630 --> 00:43:47.450
So in the script, there was a scene, Greg, that as the boat is pulling away, Toby's car suddenly drives up.

1034
00:43:48.310 --> 00:43:54.530
And he, and Michael yells at him from the ship, oh, come on, what's up?

1035
00:43:54.590 --> 00:43:58.070
And he's like, I'm sorry, there was an accident or there was traffic.

1036
00:43:58.390 --> 00:44:00.490
And then Michael says, we're not going back.

1037
00:44:00.610 --> 00:44:01.410
We're not going back for him.

1038
00:44:01.450 --> 00:44:02.090
Just leave him.

1039
00:44:02.330 --> 00:44:06.730
And then Paul turns to camera as Toby and he kind of gives a little wink.

1040
00:44:06.770 --> 00:44:07.410
Yeah, it's a big smile.

1041
00:44:07.430 --> 00:44:08.250
Yeah, like he did it on purpose.

1042
00:44:08.430 --> 00:44:10.070
But you can find that in the deleted scenes.

1043
00:44:10.110 --> 00:44:10.710
We shot it.

1044
00:44:10.790 --> 00:44:11.030
Yes.

1045
00:44:11.150 --> 00:44:11.710
We shot it.

1046
00:44:11.730 --> 00:44:12.310
And it was very nice.

1047
00:44:12.310 --> 00:44:15.450
And that is why Toby is not on the boat for anyone who was wondering.

1048
00:44:15.950 --> 00:44:16.570
Maybe we should take a boat.

1049
00:44:16.570 --> 00:44:23.230
There's a lot of excuses for why Toby wouldn't be on things so that he could help out as a writer and producer.

1050
00:44:23.790 --> 00:44:25.670
Toby and BJ and Mindy.

1051
00:44:25.870 --> 00:44:25.990
Yeah.

1052
00:44:25.990 --> 00:44:28.410
Like they sat in the, you know, Mindy was in the annex.

1053
00:44:28.730 --> 00:44:28.780
Yeah.

1054
00:44:28.850 --> 00:44:29.170
Things like that.

1055
00:44:29.170 --> 00:44:35.410
I thought I was so clever in season one making them actors so that they would have this experience of being actors.

1056
00:44:35.430 --> 00:44:41.530
And then at some point in season two, I'm looking around and 50% of the writers are gone because they're on set.

1057
00:44:41.530 --> 00:44:42.690
And I'm like, oh, what have I done?

1058
00:44:45.230 --> 00:44:45.670
Okay.

1059
00:44:45.810 --> 00:44:49.190
At seven minutes, 55 seconds, Rob Riggle breaks.

1060
00:44:49.390 --> 00:44:50.490
He totally breaks.

1061
00:44:50.710 --> 00:44:50.870
What?

1062
00:44:51.010 --> 00:44:51.750
We talked about it.

1063
00:44:51.810 --> 00:44:53.530
It just, it stayed in the shot.

1064
00:44:53.770 --> 00:44:58.150
So Rob as Captain Jack is going over the safety features of the boat.

1065
00:44:58.730 --> 00:45:02.170
And Michael really wants to hijack this speech and give it.

1066
00:45:02.310 --> 00:45:03.670
And he can't.

1067
00:45:03.770 --> 00:45:07.210
So he just starts doing like the flight attendant, like pointing to the exits.

1068
00:45:07.430 --> 00:45:07.570
Yeah.

1069
00:45:07.770 --> 00:45:08.830
And Rob just breaks.

1070
00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:10.990
He just starts laughing, but he just keeps going.

1071
00:45:11.530 --> 00:45:13.130
And then Rain is Dwight laughs.

1072
00:45:13.330 --> 00:45:15.390
And so it just sort of like, but it stays in.

1073
00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:16.490
Oh, that's amazing.

1074
00:45:16.790 --> 00:45:18.870
But Rob was like, yeah, I just laughed.

1075
00:45:18.890 --> 00:45:21.090
And I, but I just kept talking through my laugh.

1076
00:45:21.370 --> 00:45:23.450
When Steve is doing his dancing.

1077
00:45:23.730 --> 00:45:24.130
Yes.

1078
00:45:24.330 --> 00:45:32.990
That I remember like trying to build the reactions because everybody's supposed to be like unimpressed and just, you know, straight faced.

1079
00:45:32.990 --> 00:45:39.590
And, uh, it was almost impossible because everybody was breaking and made him do that over and over and over again.

1080
00:45:39.750 --> 00:45:41.030
For so long.

1081
00:45:41.110 --> 00:45:41.230
Yeah.

1082
00:45:41.230 --> 00:45:47.610
That's, I remember, I was, I remember when we rewatched it, I just wrote, oh my gosh, Michael's dance because he danced for so long.

1083
00:45:47.610 --> 00:45:51.350
In my journal, I said that we started taping that around 2 a.m.

1084
00:45:51.470 --> 00:45:53.070
Like it was really late.

1085
00:45:53.510 --> 00:45:53.810
Yeah.

1086
00:45:54.070 --> 00:45:56.970
A fan question from Tara Leitchkey.

1087
00:45:57.730 --> 00:46:00.350
Was Steve's dance choreographed?

1088
00:46:01.110 --> 00:46:02.270
I don't think so.

1089
00:46:02.490 --> 00:46:04.190
I really think he improvised that.

1090
00:46:04.210 --> 00:46:06.190
I don't remember it being in the script.

1091
00:46:06.410 --> 00:46:08.970
I think he just kind of went for it.

1092
00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:15.210
I have the script at home for this episode and I read it and there is no direction for Michael's dance in the script.

1093
00:46:15.430 --> 00:46:18.790
It just says, pan over to see Michael doing a weird dance.

1094
00:46:18.950 --> 00:46:19.270
Yeah.

1095
00:46:19.410 --> 00:46:19.790
That's it.

1096
00:46:19.930 --> 00:46:22.410
That's as much as there was.

1097
00:46:22.910 --> 00:46:24.670
Another fan question about the dance.

1098
00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:32.270
Steve Dresser says, how many takes of that superb dance did it take to get Steve's full moves?

1099
00:46:32.530 --> 00:46:34.370
I'm going to say about six, I think.

1100
00:46:34.510 --> 00:46:34.650
Yeah.

1101
00:46:34.850 --> 00:46:35.470
What do you think?

1102
00:46:35.590 --> 00:46:36.170
What do you remember?

1103
00:46:36.470 --> 00:46:37.590
That seems about right.

1104
00:46:37.830 --> 00:46:40.950
I can't remember when he started slapping his legs.

1105
00:46:40.950 --> 00:46:41.950
Yeah, that was amazing.

1106
00:46:42.070 --> 00:46:43.830
If that was early on or later.

1107
00:46:44.110 --> 00:46:44.890
And the worm.

1108
00:46:45.050 --> 00:46:45.890
I mean, they were different.

1109
00:46:45.890 --> 00:46:50.510
I feel like there were different versions of the dance and then we kind of made the highlight reel.

1110
00:46:50.770 --> 00:46:50.950
Yes.

1111
00:46:54.810 --> 00:46:56.790
Of the dancing, if you want to.

1112
00:46:57.010 --> 00:47:00.050
I remember when Ken finally was like, okay, cut.

1113
00:47:00.390 --> 00:47:02.810
Steve was on the ground doing the last worm.

1114
00:47:02.810 --> 00:47:03.590
Yeah, that was the last one.

1115
00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:03.830
Yeah.

1116
00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:10.870
And he got up and everyone applauded for him and you guys came over and gave him a hug because he had really gone for it.

1117
00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:11.170
That was exhausting.

1118
00:47:11.350 --> 00:47:12.270
Yeah, he completely did.

1119
00:47:12.410 --> 00:47:13.010
That was amazing.

1120
00:47:13.210 --> 00:47:14.430
Here's a fan question.

1121
00:47:14.770 --> 00:47:19.010
Was there music playing when Michael danced or was it added later?

1122
00:47:19.810 --> 00:47:22.070
My memory is that it was added later.

1123
00:47:22.070 --> 00:47:32.330
I mean, I feel like what we often did was we would do one take with the music and then somebody would have a little earwig, you know, and they would say,

1124
00:47:32.430 --> 00:47:33.650
do you want to do it with an earwig?

1125
00:47:33.690 --> 00:47:38.470
And a lot of effort had gone into making a tiny invisible thing that could play the soundtrack.

1126
00:47:38.730 --> 00:47:40.030
And then the actor would go, no, I don't need it.

1127
00:47:40.790 --> 00:47:44.130
And then it was due to the next five takes just for memory.

1128
00:47:44.290 --> 00:47:46.210
That's how I feel like it probably went down.

1129
00:47:46.370 --> 00:47:50.190
I do remember some fun facts about the band on Booze Cruise.

1130
00:47:50.190 --> 00:47:52.310
Yes, we had a lot of questions about the band.

1131
00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:53.410
Yes, exactly.

1132
00:47:53.630 --> 00:48:03.770
Because my friend Bob Thiel, Jr., as you were pointing out on your card, who did our theme song, was in the band.

1133
00:48:04.110 --> 00:48:13.750
And three out of the four band people were the guys who played our theme song, Hal Cragen and Dylan also.

1134
00:48:15.090 --> 00:48:18.310
Dylan O'Brien, who's a big songwriter in L.A.

1135
00:48:18.310 --> 00:48:24.270
And these guys were friends of mine, or Bob was.

1136
00:48:24.490 --> 00:48:38.030
And the theme song for The Office was written by Jay Ferguson, but he sent over a version of it on Synthesizer, which was, you know, a little – it was great.

1137
00:48:38.050 --> 00:48:39.130
It was obviously a great song.

1138
00:48:39.290 --> 00:48:39.670
Loved it.

1139
00:48:39.670 --> 00:48:42.750
But it didn't sound – it didn't have the energy that you'd want.

1140
00:48:43.830 --> 00:48:55.370
And so Bob had this kind of rockin' band that he put together of his friends, and we went and recorded the theme song with all live instruments in a session.

1141
00:48:55.730 --> 00:48:55.850
Yeah.

1142
00:48:56.190 --> 00:48:57.190
That is so cool.

1143
00:48:57.190 --> 00:48:58.550
I did not know that.

1144
00:48:58.710 --> 00:48:58.850
Yeah.

1145
00:48:59.110 --> 00:49:00.190
That is so cool.

1146
00:49:00.190 --> 00:49:03.430
And then he became – you know, then he called his band the Scrantones.

1147
00:49:03.730 --> 00:49:03.970
Yeah.

1148
00:49:04.050 --> 00:49:08.890
And then he came to Scranton with us and would play at our parties often.

1149
00:49:09.190 --> 00:49:16.410
And he was in the – I had a fan write in and say that he's also in Phyllis' Wedding with Kevin in Scrantonicity.

1150
00:49:16.570 --> 00:49:17.930
I had a fan as well.

1151
00:49:18.050 --> 00:49:26.190
Vanessa McLoad said that she noticed that at least one of the band members shows up later in Scrantonicity at Phyllis' Wedding.

1152
00:49:26.250 --> 00:49:27.030
Yeah, it's Bob.

1153
00:49:27.030 --> 00:49:33.310
Well, Bob – you know, Bob also did music – the music for Sons of Anarchy and the Mayans.

1154
00:49:33.490 --> 00:49:34.910
So he's quite talented.

1155
00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:35.690
He's so talented.

1156
00:49:35.950 --> 00:49:36.000
Yeah.

1157
00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:38.130
I have it 11 minutes, 14 seconds.

1158
00:49:38.410 --> 00:49:39.810
This is really crazy.

1159
00:49:40.150 --> 00:49:40.200
What?

1160
00:49:40.200 --> 00:49:41.250
If you know these three people.

1161
00:49:41.650 --> 00:49:44.790
Phyllis, Kevin, and Angela are dancing together.

1162
00:49:45.550 --> 00:49:46.030
Oh.

1163
00:49:46.230 --> 00:49:46.510
What?

1164
00:49:47.430 --> 00:49:48.130
I'm telling you.

1165
00:49:48.130 --> 00:49:52.130
Isn't there a shot of Phyllis dancing with Creed at one point?

1166
00:49:52.130 --> 00:49:52.510
Yes.

1167
00:49:52.570 --> 00:49:52.850
Yes.

1168
00:49:53.110 --> 00:49:54.710
Towards the end, there is.

1169
00:49:54.830 --> 00:49:55.590
They're dancing together.

1170
00:49:55.590 --> 00:50:02.710
But we're doing that awkward dance where, like – I almost feel like in eighth grade, we're like a group of girls dance together.

1171
00:50:03.150 --> 00:50:04.850
Except it's me, Phyllis, and Kevin.

1172
00:50:05.530 --> 00:50:07.990
And we're just kind of doing, like, a little sway like that.

1173
00:50:08.070 --> 00:50:09.170
It's just very funny to me.

1174
00:50:09.330 --> 00:50:15.370
Well, I don't know if you remember, Greg, but there was a runner – I read it in the script, and then I went back and I saw it in the deleted scenes.

1175
00:50:15.370 --> 00:50:20.550
There was this whole runner through this episode that Oscar is a really mean drunk.

1176
00:50:21.050 --> 00:50:21.450
Yes.

1177
00:50:21.590 --> 00:50:22.650
I'm glad we cut that.

1178
00:50:23.030 --> 00:50:23.350
Yeah.

1179
00:50:23.490 --> 00:50:24.230
I didn't like that.

1180
00:50:24.390 --> 00:50:26.510
I reread the script, too, and I was like, ooh.

1181
00:50:26.870 --> 00:50:28.310
Glad we didn't use this.

1182
00:50:28.650 --> 00:50:32.890
Where he's just – he's getting drunk, and then he's going around, and he's insulting all of you.

1183
00:50:33.010 --> 00:50:35.930
So, that is probably why Oscar is not dancing with you guys.

1184
00:50:36.090 --> 00:50:37.330
And also, later –

1185
00:50:37.330 --> 00:50:38.430
He's isolated himself.

1186
00:50:38.430 --> 00:50:41.750
Yeah, later, it's just Kevin and I in the booth, and that's probably why.

1187
00:50:41.930 --> 00:50:44.410
Because Oscar's off, like, talking about you.

1188
00:50:44.590 --> 00:50:45.250
Oh, I forgot about that.

1189
00:50:45.350 --> 00:50:50.930
Well, I want to go back to the scene with Jim, Katie, Pam, and Roy sitting around the table.

1190
00:50:51.170 --> 00:50:51.570
Great.

1191
00:50:52.550 --> 00:50:57.070
Katie makes a comment where she says, ah, we're at the cool table.

1192
00:50:57.630 --> 00:51:01.450
And then they all talk about what they were like in high school, except Jim.

1193
00:51:01.450 --> 00:51:09.210
We don't hear about Jim, but we find out that Katie was a cheerleader, and that Pam was artsy-fartsy.

1194
00:51:09.330 --> 00:51:09.990
Thanks, Roy.

1195
00:51:10.270 --> 00:51:12.430
And that Roy played sports.

1196
00:51:12.830 --> 00:51:15.950
And then Amy does her little cheer.

1197
00:51:16.190 --> 00:51:19.670
Amy, as Katie, does her awesome cheer, and that was not in the script.

1198
00:51:19.990 --> 00:51:25.070
We found out on the set that Amy Adams was a cheerleader, and she knew a bunch of cheers,

1199
00:51:25.290 --> 00:51:29.830
and she did – you had her do little cheers for us to react to.

1200
00:51:29.830 --> 00:51:31.990
So Amy and I did a few cheers together.

1201
00:51:32.470 --> 00:51:34.350
We were all really punchy, you guys.

1202
00:51:34.510 --> 00:51:36.470
It was, like, 3 in the morning, you know?

1203
00:51:36.550 --> 00:51:39.190
So do you remember – because I remember when we were doing that scene,

1204
00:51:39.670 --> 00:51:44.230
and Amy started to do the cheerleader stuff, we did a couple of takes of it,

1205
00:51:44.270 --> 00:51:48.330
and then Ken wanted to move on, and something was bothering me,

1206
00:51:48.850 --> 00:51:50.950
and I didn't want to move on from that scene.

1207
00:51:50.950 --> 00:51:55.210
And I realized that the balance – she kind of shifted the balance.

1208
00:51:55.510 --> 00:51:57.450
Because Amy Adams is obviously very charismatic.

1209
00:51:57.810 --> 00:51:57.990
Yeah.

1210
00:51:58.170 --> 00:51:59.030
You know, beautiful actress.

1211
00:51:59.030 --> 00:52:05.810
And she did that really fun, cute cheerleader thing, and then we were going to move on.

1212
00:52:05.910 --> 00:52:13.290
And I realized, like, Pam is just basically not participating on the same level here.

1213
00:52:13.430 --> 00:52:19.810
She's kind of denying the cheerleader thing, but she isn't doing her own charismatic thing.

1214
00:52:19.890 --> 00:52:23.950
And we went back, and at 9.09, I went back to you, and I said,

1215
00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:25.110
you've got to kick this up a notch.

1216
00:52:25.190 --> 00:52:26.550
Do you remember that?

1217
00:52:26.550 --> 00:52:26.850
Yeah.

1218
00:52:27.010 --> 00:52:31.090
And then you kind of mocked her cheerleader thing, and you did that, and I was like, yeah.

1219
00:52:31.330 --> 00:52:32.890
Yeah, because that was not in the script.

1220
00:52:32.950 --> 00:52:33.570
That wasn't, no.

1221
00:52:33.570 --> 00:52:35.510
That was added in the moment.

1222
00:52:35.710 --> 00:52:35.890
Yeah.

1223
00:52:35.930 --> 00:52:40.170
And that's – yeah, I kind of flirt with Jim there.

1224
00:52:40.190 --> 00:52:40.330
Yeah.

1225
00:52:40.330 --> 00:52:40.410
I know.

1226
00:52:40.410 --> 00:52:42.190
We have a private moment at the table.

1227
00:52:42.190 --> 00:52:46.130
I thought that was, like, so bold with Roy sitting next to you.

1228
00:52:46.250 --> 00:52:52.570
Like, when I watched it again, I was like, wait, she's flirting with Jim, with Roy next

1229
00:52:52.570 --> 00:52:55.390
to her, and also kind of making fun of Katie.

1230
00:52:55.530 --> 00:52:55.770
Yeah.

1231
00:52:55.930 --> 00:52:56.070
Yeah.

1232
00:52:56.070 --> 00:52:57.550
I was like, Pam's having a moment.

1233
00:52:57.830 --> 00:53:02.510
Well, I think it was an important connection there, Greg, because it is certainly going to

1234
00:53:02.510 --> 00:53:09.730
help give Jim the courage to do what he's going to attempt to do, which is tell Pam his feelings.

1235
00:53:09.990 --> 00:53:10.270
Yeah.

1236
00:53:10.390 --> 00:53:15.550
I think without that little moment of them bonding over how ridiculous their significant

1237
00:53:15.550 --> 00:53:20.390
others are being, it's like you need to see them as that bonded couple.

1238
00:53:20.870 --> 00:53:21.370
Yeah.

1239
00:53:21.490 --> 00:53:25.590
So it doesn't come so out of the blue that Jim would think, this could be my moment.

1240
00:53:25.590 --> 00:53:28.810
Well, also, Pam really gives him an opening.

1241
00:53:29.030 --> 00:53:29.530
Yeah.

1242
00:53:29.530 --> 00:53:30.730
On the top of the boat there.

1243
00:53:30.730 --> 00:53:31.270
She does.

1244
00:53:31.630 --> 00:53:32.130
Yeah.

1245
00:53:32.430 --> 00:53:38.990
That, to me, the Jim thing of it was she's engaged, and he wants to be a good person and,

1246
00:53:38.990 --> 00:53:41.170
you know, respect the fact that she's engaged.

1247
00:53:41.570 --> 00:53:49.310
But he's also, like, there's all these clues coming in that there could be something, and

1248
00:53:49.310 --> 00:53:50.490
he doesn't go for it.

1249
00:53:50.570 --> 00:53:54.970
And it's very frustrating to watch.

1250
00:53:54.970 --> 00:53:55.530
It is.

1251
00:53:55.630 --> 00:53:58.730
It's so painful to watch, but also feel so honeSt.

1252
00:53:59.030 --> 00:54:00.710
Because that's how you would be.

1253
00:54:00.870 --> 00:54:04.130
You wouldn't, you'd want to say everything, and you wouldn't be able to.

1254
00:54:04.430 --> 00:54:13.430
Well, I remember, to me, that was probably one of the most personal and sort of raw things

1255
00:54:13.430 --> 00:54:15.630
that I would have put into a script.

1256
00:54:15.630 --> 00:54:25.350
Because it did not feel like a very, like, noble Jim, successful social guy moment.

1257
00:54:25.350 --> 00:54:30.770
It felt really like, ugh, people are going to hate him for this or disrespect him or whatever.

1258
00:54:30.770 --> 00:54:34.330
So I kind of felt it was risky to put it out there.

1259
00:54:35.750 --> 00:54:36.350
I don't know.

1260
00:54:36.410 --> 00:54:40.330
It was very hard also in the edit to keep it that long.

1261
00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:45.690
I mean, we tried to make the same length that you guys really did in the scene.

1262
00:54:45.930 --> 00:54:48.570
And there was a lot of urge to, like, trim that up.

1263
00:54:48.650 --> 00:54:51.410
That's an awful long time to have people not talk.

1264
00:54:51.410 --> 00:54:52.170
Yes.

1265
00:54:52.410 --> 00:54:53.530
Let's talk about that.

1266
00:54:53.810 --> 00:54:54.250
But it's so beautiful.

1267
00:54:54.530 --> 00:54:55.330
It's so beautiful.

1268
00:54:55.710 --> 00:54:57.690
Pam and Jim are on the deck of that boat.

1269
00:54:57.750 --> 00:55:01.170
And Pam does give him an opening.

1270
00:55:01.490 --> 00:55:03.790
She says, sometimes I just don't get Roy.

1271
00:55:04.370 --> 00:55:12.490
And this is the first time we've seen her complain about Roy to Jim, to disparage her relationship.

1272
00:55:13.550 --> 00:55:14.110
Really.

1273
00:55:14.350 --> 00:55:16.190
It's the first time she's verbalized it.

1274
00:55:16.270 --> 00:55:17.450
I don't, I'm pretty sure.

1275
00:55:17.650 --> 00:55:21.050
And then it is 27 seconds of silence.

1276
00:55:21.750 --> 00:55:28.830
And a fan wrote in, Anthony Sneed, said, what was the motivation behind that awkward silence?

1277
00:55:29.150 --> 00:55:31.210
There's a lot going on in those 27 seconds.

1278
00:55:31.290 --> 00:55:33.810
Just because people aren't talking doesn't mean nothing's going on.

1279
00:55:33.890 --> 00:55:45.710
And I think that you look at his face and, you know, you can see him trying out different things and thinking and being, you know, urged to go forward and blocked and, you know.

1280
00:55:45.710 --> 00:55:57.510
And then I think also on your face, there's all this kind of like disappointment that he's not going to do anything and face saving when you're like, well, I'm cold.

1281
00:55:57.710 --> 00:56:00.110
And kind of, it was kind of good that it was cold actually.

1282
00:56:00.730 --> 00:56:07.910
And we really did shoot it out in the harbor because I think you wouldn't have been able to, like, your nose was red.

1283
00:56:08.050 --> 00:56:08.210
Yeah.

1284
00:56:08.210 --> 00:56:08.530
You know what I mean?

1285
00:56:08.730 --> 00:56:09.730
I was cold.

1286
00:56:09.770 --> 00:56:10.650
Yeah, you were cold.

1287
00:56:10.890 --> 00:56:11.910
Yeah, I believed it.

1288
00:56:12.030 --> 00:56:14.970
Now, Greg, it wasn't in the script that there would be a big pause.

1289
00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:19.570
It didn't say there's a long, big pause.

1290
00:56:19.850 --> 00:56:20.010
Yeah.

1291
00:56:20.010 --> 00:56:23.770
It just says that they look at each other and then finally Pam says.

1292
00:56:24.390 --> 00:56:24.610
Yeah.

1293
00:56:25.210 --> 00:56:33.290
But I remember on the day you really encouraging us to take our time and don't be afraid to just look at each other.

1294
00:56:33.950 --> 00:56:43.490
Well, I mean, that was part of, I think, what made the show so special was that, and so different from what was on TV, you know,

1295
00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:50.710
was that moments of behavior were really important more so than jokes and setups and punchlines and lines and stuff.

1296
00:56:51.510 --> 00:56:56.650
And so, and I think that came out of appreciating what's good about a documentary,

1297
00:56:56.950 --> 00:57:07.990
and documentaries just are all about finding the truth in what people are doing and leaning in and no matter what they think they're doing or what they say they're doing,

1298
00:57:08.710 --> 00:57:11.170
what are their actions show you that they're doing.

1299
00:57:11.170 --> 00:57:21.010
And this was like a good romantic example of, you know, it all being in between the lines and it all being on the faces and not in the script, so.

1300
00:57:21.190 --> 00:57:22.610
That is so smartly said.

1301
00:57:22.750 --> 00:57:26.670
You guys, should we talk about what else was going on during this scene?

1302
00:57:27.110 --> 00:57:30.090
Do you mean when half of the cast got lost at sea?

1303
00:57:30.650 --> 00:57:31.090
Yes.

1304
00:57:32.590 --> 00:57:34.230
All right, so here's the deal.

1305
00:57:34.470 --> 00:57:37.590
When we started this scene, I think we knew it was going to take a while.

1306
00:57:37.590 --> 00:57:47.490
Well, and so they told the other cast members that they could leave for the day, but the only way to get off the boat, because we weren't going to take the boat back and dock it.

1307
00:57:47.590 --> 00:57:52.870
No, there were these little dinghies that would pull up, and you would get in the little boat, and it would take you to shore.

1308
00:57:53.010 --> 00:57:53.410
Exactly.

1309
00:57:54.110 --> 00:57:56.350
Well, that did not go as planned.

1310
00:57:56.730 --> 00:57:57.770
No, it didn't.

1311
00:57:57.870 --> 00:58:00.750
I remember getting this panicky radio thing.

1312
00:58:01.210 --> 00:58:03.490
The boat with Phyllis on it is gone.

1313
00:58:03.830 --> 00:58:05.470
Look, look, look at my card.

1314
00:58:05.630 --> 00:58:06.450
You were on the boat too.

1315
00:58:06.450 --> 00:58:07.930
I was stranded on the boat.

1316
00:58:08.130 --> 00:58:09.790
Yeah, and Brian too, right?

1317
00:58:09.890 --> 00:58:12.490
And Rain, and I wrote about it in my journal.

1318
00:58:12.710 --> 00:58:12.930
Yeah.

1319
00:58:13.590 --> 00:58:14.450
It was crazy.

1320
00:58:14.630 --> 00:58:17.430
Well, for some reason, it fixated on me that it was Phyllis.

1321
00:58:17.570 --> 00:58:19.870
That Phyllis was, that we had lost Phyllis.

1322
00:58:19.870 --> 00:58:22.230
She seems like the most vulnerable of the group.

1323
00:58:22.370 --> 00:58:22.530
Yeah, yeah.

1324
00:58:22.530 --> 00:58:23.630
Right, kind Phyllis.

1325
00:58:23.690 --> 00:58:27.210
Yeah, so I wrote her about it, and she-

1326
00:58:27.210 --> 00:58:28.330
I can't wait to hear what she says.

1327
00:58:28.330 --> 00:58:35.350
Yeah, well, she says the first memory, she said it was about 2.30 in the morning, and she was waiting to leave the ship,

1328
00:58:35.350 --> 00:58:43.730
and her first memory was seeing Steve Carell on a tiny little boat, holding onto some kind of a pole with no life jacket, disappearing into the night.

1329
00:58:43.730 --> 00:58:53.510
And she remembers thinking that if NBC knew that their star was just, didn't have a life jacket and was, you know, being pulled off back to the harbor, they would be upset.

1330
00:58:53.510 --> 00:59:02.510
And then she got on the boat, and she thought it was Oscar, Brian, and somebody from the wardrobe department.

1331
00:59:03.390 --> 00:59:05.290
I wrote about it the day of.

1332
00:59:05.330 --> 00:59:05.510
But that was you.

1333
00:59:05.750 --> 00:59:05.910
Yeah.

1334
00:59:06.290 --> 00:59:08.090
No, she says, I'm not sure if Angela was there or not.

1335
00:59:08.450 --> 00:59:11.210
And then she talks, everything was fine pulling away.

1336
00:59:11.370 --> 00:59:16.590
We were enjoying the smell of the ocean, the lights in the distance, and then all of a sudden, nothing.

1337
00:59:16.590 --> 00:59:17.610
The engine died.

1338
00:59:18.230 --> 00:59:19.190
So you were there.

1339
00:59:19.190 --> 00:59:24.390
So this is what I wrote the day after we filmed the scene.

1340
00:59:24.750 --> 00:59:25.190
All right.

1341
00:59:25.310 --> 00:59:31.430
I wrote, one of the coolest things that happened to me while we shot this episode happened the last night, or I guess I should say the morning of.

1342
00:59:31.590 --> 00:59:35.050
Rain, Phyllis, and Brian and I were all wrapped at 5.30 a.m.

1343
00:59:35.430 --> 00:59:42.470
We were on the main boat, but they said they had more shooting to do so we could stay on the big boat and, like, sleep in a booth.

1344
00:59:43.050 --> 00:59:43.290
Yeah.

1345
00:59:43.290 --> 00:59:49.670
Or they said they had these little rubber boats that would pull up next to the big boat that we could get on and they would take us to shore.

1346
00:59:50.290 --> 00:59:52.050
So we said, okay, we'll do that.

1347
00:59:52.130 --> 00:59:55.290
They said it's about a 15-minute boat ride on the little rubber dinghy.

1348
00:59:56.230 --> 00:59:57.390
So we decided to do it.

1349
00:59:57.510 --> 00:59:59.630
It was really tricky getting on the rubber boat.

1350
00:59:59.810 --> 01:00:00.090
Yeah.

1351
01:00:00.290 --> 01:00:03.430
We just had to climb out a ladder off the side of the boat.

1352
01:00:03.850 --> 01:00:04.530
And these guys kind of-

1353
01:00:04.530 --> 01:00:05.090
It was very unstable.

1354
01:00:05.390 --> 01:00:05.610
Very-

1355
01:00:05.610 --> 01:00:07.750
Phyllis said that at one point she had to switch boats.

1356
01:00:07.750 --> 01:00:09.430
She was terrified of falling in.

1357
01:00:09.430 --> 01:00:09.870
Yes.

1358
01:00:10.030 --> 01:00:21.110
And I said, okay, the little rubber boat, picture the kind of, like, rubber boat that the Navy SEALs do the backflips off of with the little, like, outboat.

1359
01:00:21.210 --> 01:00:22.130
That's what this looked like.

1360
01:00:22.430 --> 01:00:29.810
So we're lowered down into it, and it was me, Rain, Jen, one of our wardrobe gals, Phyllis, and Brian.

1361
01:00:30.090 --> 01:00:31.970
It was tiny and really bouncy.

1362
01:00:31.970 --> 01:00:32.710
We took off.

1363
01:00:32.770 --> 01:00:35.550
It was dark out, and the ocean looked like black glass.

1364
01:00:35.730 --> 01:00:36.870
It was very peaceful.

1365
01:00:37.170 --> 01:00:38.330
Oh, it's so poetic.

1366
01:00:38.330 --> 01:00:38.830
Yeah, yeah.

1367
01:00:39.050 --> 01:00:39.530
Sorry.

1368
01:00:40.030 --> 01:00:45.130
About five minutes into our ride, our engine made a weird whirring sound and stopped working.

1369
01:00:45.810 --> 01:00:49.710
And then we heard the boat driver radio and say, the engine's dead.

1370
01:00:49.710 --> 01:00:50.990
You need to come get us.

1371
01:00:51.310 --> 01:00:54.270
Brian started laughing and said, you've got to be kidding me.

1372
01:00:54.970 --> 01:00:58.610
Then we just floated in the darkness, which felt like for a while.

1373
01:00:59.310 --> 01:01:01.510
It was quiet and peaceful.

1374
01:01:01.750 --> 01:01:02.790
It turned into the Titanic.

1375
01:01:03.070 --> 01:01:03.550
I know.

1376
01:01:03.730 --> 01:01:04.390
I know.

1377
01:01:04.390 --> 01:01:13.370
Oh, and then we, another rubber boat pulled up, and then we had to just straddle from one rubber boat with just two guys kind of holding it together into the other one.

1378
01:01:13.510 --> 01:01:14.650
This was very tricky.

1379
01:01:15.210 --> 01:01:17.430
We slowly made our way past the Queen Mary.

1380
01:01:17.630 --> 01:01:24.890
Picture an old cruise ship that looks like the Titanic, and there was a huge plume of fire coming from a refinery behind it.

1381
01:01:25.270 --> 01:01:27.270
Rain said to me, this is so surreal.

1382
01:01:27.810 --> 01:01:29.570
And that's exactly what it was.

1383
01:01:29.790 --> 01:01:32.930
You know, the wheels really came off when we went on locations.

1384
01:01:33.190 --> 01:01:34.730
We talk about this a little bit.

1385
01:01:34.730 --> 01:01:39.890
I mean, you know, this is for season nine, but, you know, death bus almost killed us all.

1386
01:01:40.270 --> 01:01:40.550
Yes.

1387
01:01:40.690 --> 01:01:41.210
Work bus.

1388
01:01:41.310 --> 01:01:41.570
Work bus.

1389
01:01:41.610 --> 01:01:42.150
I should say.

1390
01:01:42.590 --> 01:01:44.630
We've called it death bus for so long.

1391
01:01:44.770 --> 01:01:46.170
The cast calls it death bus.

1392
01:01:46.450 --> 01:01:46.970
All right.

1393
01:01:47.050 --> 01:01:48.430
Well, we'll take a break, and we'll be back.

1394
01:01:48.650 --> 01:01:53.680
All right.

1395
01:01:53.680 --> 01:01:54.860
So we are back from break.

1396
01:01:55.560 --> 01:01:57.500
I have a question for you, Greg.

1397
01:01:57.660 --> 01:01:57.780
Yeah.

1398
01:01:57.920 --> 01:01:59.560
I've been waiting to ask you this.

1399
01:01:59.560 --> 01:02:09.800
I know we're sort of jumping around with time codes, but at 11 minutes, 34 seconds, there's this beautiful shot that goes outside of the boat and goes around the boat.

1400
01:02:09.960 --> 01:02:14.340
You pass Dwight, right, like steering, and it goes around the boat.

1401
01:02:14.420 --> 01:02:15.280
How did we do that?

1402
01:02:15.760 --> 01:02:17.120
That is Kent Zabornak.

1403
01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:28.940
So Kent was our line producer, and he was also in the DGA, and, you know, he managed to launch us on the budget that we had, which was tiny.

1404
01:02:29.560 --> 01:02:39.680
You know, into Long Beach Harbor on a boat for days and get hotel rooms for everybody, and he realized that we really needed a shot like that.

1405
01:02:39.840 --> 01:02:45.000
And he shot that himself with his own equipment, you know,

1406
01:02:45.160 --> 01:02:53.360
while we were shooting the rest of it because he just had an instinct that there was no way the production could get that and stop everything.

1407
01:02:53.660 --> 01:02:57.960
And, you know, so that was kind of a guerrilla move and super valuable.

1408
01:02:58.640 --> 01:03:01.980
And Kent was, you know, fantastic at doing those kind of things.

1409
01:03:02.180 --> 01:03:07.520
Like, he was always stitching together amazing stuff on our little budget.

1410
01:03:08.100 --> 01:03:09.100
That is so cool.

1411
01:03:09.220 --> 01:03:09.980
I did not know that.

1412
01:03:10.100 --> 01:03:10.300
Did you?

1413
01:03:10.460 --> 01:03:10.640
No.

1414
01:03:10.640 --> 01:03:16.300
Like, when I saw it, I was like, wait, I know Randall and Matt were inside with us.

1415
01:03:16.300 --> 01:03:17.000
Yeah, who did that, right?

1416
01:03:17.000 --> 01:03:17.680
Our camera guys.

1417
01:03:17.700 --> 01:03:18.800
I was like, how did that happen?

1418
01:03:18.800 --> 01:03:20.880
And then I was like, is that a plane?

1419
01:03:20.880 --> 01:03:22.740
I mean, like, it's in the air.

1420
01:03:22.940 --> 01:03:23.320
Like, how?

1421
01:03:23.520 --> 01:03:24.820
That, I mean, is it a drone?

1422
01:03:24.960 --> 01:03:25.700
Like, what is it?

1423
01:03:25.720 --> 01:03:26.860
He's on one of those dinghies.

1424
01:03:27.220 --> 01:03:28.960
He's on one of those Zodiac dinghies.

1425
01:03:29.000 --> 01:03:29.100
Oh, he's on a dinghy.

1426
01:03:29.120 --> 01:03:29.260
Yeah.

1427
01:03:29.400 --> 01:03:29.640
Okay.

1428
01:03:30.020 --> 01:03:30.180
Yeah.

1429
01:03:30.400 --> 01:03:30.760
All right.

1430
01:03:30.920 --> 01:03:31.200
Wow.

1431
01:03:31.420 --> 01:03:35.060
This is another question about a camera movement.

1432
01:03:35.060 --> 01:03:46.280
So, you know, after they're at the bar and they're all talking about who they would save, Jim gets up his courage and he's going to walk over to Pam and he says,

1433
01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:47.820
I would save the receptioniSt.

1434
01:03:47.920 --> 01:03:49.240
I think I need to make that clear.

1435
01:03:49.540 --> 01:03:53.960
Well, right before that, Greg Phelps noticed at 14 minutes, 37 seconds,

1436
01:03:54.120 --> 01:04:00.340
there's a continuous shot of Jim looking down the length of the boat to Pam as he thinks about who he would save.

1437
01:04:00.340 --> 01:04:05.740
The camera then zooms in on Pam at the exact moment she has a really cute laughing smile.

1438
01:04:06.180 --> 01:04:08.200
How did you time that out so perfectly?

1439
01:04:09.000 --> 01:04:13.280
He wants to know, Jenna, were you given a cue when the camera was about to zoom in on you?

1440
01:04:13.400 --> 01:04:15.500
But we used to do that stuff all the time.

1441
01:04:16.540 --> 01:04:17.720
That was, go ahead.

1442
01:04:17.720 --> 01:04:29.700
People, I think, have the wrong idea about how we did the show in a way because, like, most filmmaking is super intentional and you would say to the camera operator, okay,

1443
01:04:30.040 --> 01:04:36.300
at this signal you need to zoom in and then you'd try and get the actor to do it at exactly the right time.

1444
01:04:36.300 --> 01:04:42.440
And our show was more like the theory of natural selection, kind of, like,

1445
01:04:42.440 --> 01:04:54.100
there would be a situation and I would give notes or the directors would give notes to the camera operators that were not about what they did specifically but more about their intentions.

1446
01:04:54.100 --> 01:05:01.880
So we would say, okay, you're really interested in what he sees down at the end of the hall, so just go find it.

1447
01:05:02.120 --> 01:05:08.680
And we had all these people who'd come from reality shows who were used to finding the story on their own.

1448
01:05:09.880 --> 01:05:12.500
And that turned out great, you know.

1449
01:05:12.740 --> 01:05:15.620
And I can't really speak for Ken as the director.

1450
01:05:15.800 --> 01:05:21.020
He directed it and he might have known exactly what he was going for and kept doing it until he got it.

1451
01:05:21.020 --> 01:05:29.900
But a lot of the times there were just lots of happy accidents and then you'd tell the story through all the happy accidents and you had enough, like,

1452
01:05:30.300 --> 01:05:34.100
flexibility that you could have gone a bunch of different ways with it.

1453
01:05:34.280 --> 01:05:36.400
I think our editors don't get enough credit.

1454
01:05:36.560 --> 01:05:42.700
Well, first of all, I think our camera operators don't get enough credit for all of those happy accidents.

1455
01:05:42.880 --> 01:05:49.760
But then you would assemble those with the editors in the editing room to tell even more of a story than what was in the script.

1456
01:05:51.020 --> 01:05:51.460
Totally.

1457
01:05:51.720 --> 01:05:53.120
The editors are amazing.

1458
01:05:53.420 --> 01:05:54.800
We had fantastic editors.

1459
01:05:55.200 --> 01:05:56.060
This one was Dean.

1460
01:05:57.120 --> 01:06:01.720
Dave Rogers, as you know, I'm still working with, work with him all the time.

1461
01:06:01.960 --> 01:06:02.160
Brilliant.

1462
01:06:03.880 --> 01:06:04.780
Claire Scanlon.

1463
01:06:04.780 --> 01:06:05.560
Claire Scanlon.

1464
01:06:05.760 --> 01:06:06.360
Also brilliant.

1465
01:06:08.360 --> 01:06:21.340
And there was enormous amounts of leeway for the editors and in post because like a documentary, we shot way more stuff than we could ever possibly use.

1466
01:06:21.480 --> 01:06:29.780
And so our rough cuts were always about 38 minutes long, but the show's only 21 minutes, you know, six seconds or something like that.

1467
01:06:29.780 --> 01:06:33.220
So tons of good material was discarded.

1468
01:06:34.600 --> 01:06:37.740
And that was like a large part of my job.

1469
01:06:37.880 --> 01:06:42.400
Like I loved being on set, you know, and I also loved being with the writers.

1470
01:06:42.660 --> 01:06:51.460
But this whole other area of my job was sitting with the editors and, you know, and they're fun people too with great, you know, creative ideas.

1471
01:06:51.460 --> 01:06:57.740
And you could tell so many different versions of the show by what you left in and what you took out.

1472
01:06:58.200 --> 01:07:06.180
And like there was, and there was always a thing where you'd like, you'd go, everybody kind of agreed on the first six or eight minutes to get rid of, you know.

1473
01:07:06.360 --> 01:07:14.040
And like it probably, we all agreed like having Oscar make snarky mean comments is not great, you know, or whatever it is.

1474
01:07:14.140 --> 01:07:17.960
But then you'd get to like about 26 minutes and there was a lot of really good stuff.

1475
01:07:18.140 --> 01:07:23.540
And I used to have all these regrets about things that were great that couldn't get in the show.

1476
01:07:23.540 --> 01:07:31.360
So one of them for this, if you remember, was Creed sitting in with the band and us finding out that he'd been in the grassroots.

1477
01:07:31.660 --> 01:07:31.980
Yeah.

1478
01:07:32.400 --> 01:07:34.420
And that was like fantastic.

1479
01:07:34.420 --> 01:07:47.100
And we had all this footage and I found footage of him from, you know, late 60s and, you know, playing guitar with the grassroots on Playboy After Dark with Hugh Hefner.

1480
01:07:47.100 --> 01:07:57.240
Like all this great footage, which was going to be the B-roll under the, you know, under him talking about playing with all these greats in the late 60s.

1481
01:07:57.320 --> 01:07:58.660
And that was in for a really long time.

1482
01:07:58.700 --> 01:08:00.220
I think it's in the DVD extras.

1483
01:08:00.520 --> 01:08:00.860
It is.

1484
01:08:00.900 --> 01:08:01.920
It's in the deleted scenes.

1485
01:08:02.020 --> 01:08:02.520
It's great.

1486
01:08:02.760 --> 01:08:08.440
Michael, the guitar player is going to take a break and then Michael tries to fill in for him and he's horrible.

1487
01:08:08.700 --> 01:08:10.700
